title: Pagination and sorting
tags: Query
kind: reference
summary: sort, limit, cursor and count on POST /query — keyset pages, unknown values last, a tree_ref sorted by its whole path.

Pagination is opt-in: without `limit`, `POST /query` answers the whole result at
once, unwrapped.

|!Key |!Type |!Does |
|`limit` |int |the page size; turns pagination on |
|`cursor` |string |the previous page's `next_cursor`; absent for the first page |
|`sort` |list |the order (below); allowed without `limit` |
|`count` |bool |adds `total`, the full number of matches (needs `limit`) — exact, and cheap: the match set is a bitmap |

With `limit` the answer is wrapped:

```
{"results": [...], "next_cursor": "<token>" | null, "total": 5000}
```

`results` has the unpaginated items' shape; `next_cursor` is `null` after the last
page. `total` is the count at the time of this page — live data may move it — so
clients usually ask for it once.

!! Sort

```
"sort": [{"field": "rating", "order": "desc"}, {"field": "title"}]
```

* `order` is `asc` (the default) or `desc`. Reserved and user fields sort alike.
* The uuid is always appended as a last key, so the order is total and a page resumes exactly after its last item even among ties.
* A metarecord where the field is unknown, or holds only `nothing`, sorts ''after'' every metarecord with a value, whatever the order.
* A multi-valued field sorts on the metarecord's ''smallest'' value ascending, its ''largest'' descending.
* Values compare in their type's natural order — numbers and dates numerically, strings lexicographically, references by uuid bytes; one field has one type, so no cross-type order is needed.

A `tree_ref` sorts on the ''whole path'', compared component by component, a prefix
first: `/photos/2021/a.jpg` < `/photos/z.jpg` < `/photos-old/a.jpg` — a folder and
everything under it stay together, which comparing the path strings would not give
(`-` sorts before `/`). Internally a path is reduced to its components joined by
U+0001, below every character a name holds, so a byte comparison is the
component-wise one; the key is rebuilt from the forest, never stored — a rename would
make a stored one stale for the whole subtree. How a page is found without sorting
every match: [[Sorting and postings]].

!! Cursor

The cursor is opaque: the sort key and uuid of the last item, plus a hash of the
query and sort that produced it. Do not parse it. It is valid only for the same
query and sort — otherwise `400` `invalid cursor: it was issued for a different query
or sort` — and does not expire. Resuming is "strictly after the last item", so pages
are stable against metarecords added or removed in the part already read; a
metarecord whose sort value changes between two pages may come twice or be skipped,
which keyset pagination over live data accepts.

The hash covers the query ''as the daemon rewrote it'', so that rewrite must be
deterministic ([[How queries are evaluated]]).
